<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palazzo Madama, Turin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palazzo Madama is a palace on Piazza Castello in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> that grew from a Roman gate and a medieval castle of the Savoy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> branch of the dynasty. It takes its name from two royal widows, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christine-of-france">Christine of France</a> and Marie Jeanne Baptiste of Savoy-Nemours, who lived there as regents in the seventeenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-juvarra">Filippo Juvarra</a> gave it a baroque façade in 1718–1721. It later housed the Subalpine Senate and is now Turin's civic museum of ancient art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palazzo-madama-turin.jpg" alt="Palazzo Madama, Turin" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lurens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palazzo_Madama,_Torino_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>At the beginning of the first century BCE the site was occupied by a gate in the Roman walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Augusta Taurinorum</a>, the ancient Turin, from which the decumanus maximus, the main east-west street, set out. Two of its towers, though restored, still stand as witnesses of this original core. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> the gate served as a fortified stronghold in the city's defences.</p><p>The building then passed to the Savoia-Acaja, a secondary branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, who enlarged it into a castle in the early 14th century. A century later Ludovico of Acaja rebuilt it as a square with an inner court, a portico and a cylindrical tower at each corner, a form still clearly visible at the back of the palace. When the Acaja line died out, the castle became a residence for guests of the House of Savoy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1637 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christine-of-france">Christine of France</a>, aunt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and regent for Duke Charles Emmanuel II, made it her own residence, roofing the court and refitting the apartments. Sixty years later the second regent, Marie Jeanne of Savoy, known as Madama Reale, lived there and fixed the name Madama on it. She wished to turn it into a sumptuous royal palace and brought in artists, among whom Domenico Guidobono decorated the first-floor halls named after him: the Madama Reale's Chamber, the Chinese Cabinet and the Southern Veranda.</p><p>Juvarra's plan was for an entirely new palace in white stone, but only its front section was built. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> the palace housed the provisional French government. In the 19th century King Charles Albert made it the seat of the royal picture gallery, the Pinacoteca Regia, called the Galleria Sabauda after 1933, and later of the Subalpine Senate, the parliament of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, and of the High Court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Juvarra's front on Piazza Castello is a scenic façade only one bay deep, screening the older building behind it. A high piano nobile with arched windows is joined to a mezzanine by a giant order of composite pilasters on a rusticated ground floor; the three central bays have full columns before a glazed hall, and a balustrade with marble vases and statues crowns the whole. Since 1934 the palace has housed the City Museum of Ancient Art, a collection of paintings, statues, church ornaments, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and decorative art mostly from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> to the 18th century. In 1997 it was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> with other residences of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palazzo Madama, Turin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…with arcaded streets and squares; Guarino Guarini and later Filippo Juvarra, who designed the Basilica of Superga, the hunting lodge of Stupinigi and the façade of <b>Palazzo Madama</b>, made it one of the architectural capitals of Europe, and the Savoy residences are now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
